Here's the assert statement based on the rules and tips provided:

<code>assert \forall int i; index <= i && i < \old(this.size()); (this.get(i) == null) == (\old(this.get(i+1)) == null) && (this.get(i) != null) => this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i+1)));</code>

However, this statement is not entirely accurate because it incorrectly compares the current element at index `i` with the old element at index `i+1`. Instead, we should compare the current element at index `i+1` with the old element at index `i`.

Let's correct this:

<code>assert \forall int i; index <= i && i < \old(this.size()); (this.get(i+1) == null) == (\old(this.get(i)) == null) && (this.get(i+1) != null) => this.get(i+1).equals(\old(this.get(i)));</code>

But since we are shifting elements to the right, the correct comparison should be between the current element at index `i` and the old element at index `i-1` for indices greater than the specified index. Let's adjust the assertion accordingly:

<code>assert \forall int i; index < i && i <= \old(this.size()); (this.get(i) == null) == (\old(this.get(i-1)) == null) && (this.get(i) != null) => this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i-1)));</code>

This ensures that for every valid index from the specified index + 1 to the old size of the list, the element at the current position is either null if the old element at the previous position was null, or it equals the old element at the previous position.

So the final assert statement is:

<code>assert \forall int i; index < i && i <= \old(this.size()); (this.get(i) == null) == (\old(this.get(i-1)) == null) && (this.get(i) != null) => this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i-1)));</code>
